  <conditions attached="1">
   <condition type="1">
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Consistent with the Article III of the SBAA [or the Supplemental Provisions to the Project Document], the responsibility for the safety and security of the Implementing Partner and its personnel and property, and of UNDP&#x2019;s property in the Implementing Partner&#x2019;s custody, rests with the Implementing Partner.  To this end, the Implementing Partner shall: a)	put in place an appropriate security plan and maintain the security plan, taking into account the security situation in the country where the project is being carried; b)	assume all risks and liabilities related to the Implementing Partner&#x2019;s security, and the full implementation of the security plan.</narrative>
   </condition>
   <condition type="1">
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Social and environmental sustainability will be enhanced through application of the UNDP Social and Environmental Standards (http://www.undp.org/ses) and related Accountability Mechanism (http://www.undp.org/secu-srm).</narrative>
   </condition>
  </conditions>
  <result aggregation-status="1" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual Output Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator ascending="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output Indicator</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.1: Strengthened institutional and regulatory systems for climate-responsive planning and development. 
[Scale: 1= Not adequately (up to 25%);  2= Very partially (26%-50%);  3= Partially (51%-75%);  4= Largely (75%-100%)]
1.2: Number of policies introduced to address GLOF risks or adjusted to incorporate GLOF risks.
2.1: Number of vulnerable households in KP and GB covered by a GLOF early warning system. 
2.2: Number of Community-based organisations trained in the operation and maintenance of the EWS. 
2.3: Number of physical assets constructed to withstand the effects of GLOF events.
2.4. Number of small-scale community driven initiatives financed through CBDRM funds</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline year="2017">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.1: Scale 1 (National, provincial and local disaster management institutions and development planners are unable to design, finance and analyze GLOF risk reduction measures on the basis of reliable, comprehensive information.)
1.2: 2 (Only 2 comprehensive disaster management guidelines exist for the KP and GB regions.)
2.1: 0 (Vulnerable households are not able to receive and react to GLOF early warning messages.)
2.2: 2 ( 2 GLOF early warning system for KP and GB in place)
2.3: 0 (No physical structures in place to withstand the effect of GLOF events.)
2.4.  0 (No financial capacity of local communities to prepare and respond to GLOF events)</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2020-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.1: Scale 4 [ By the end of Year 3, 100% of the national and 90% of district and community authorities in the KP and GB regions are able to prioritize and plan measures to minimize potential losses from GLOFs]
1.2. 4 [Number of policies introduced to address GLOF risks or adjusted to incorporate GLOF risks]
2.1: 696,342 people [348,171 men, 348,171 women [Planned for end of project].
2.2: 24 [By the end of the project, at least 24 CBOs are trained in the operation and maintenance of the EWS and ensure its continued functionality].
2.3: 250 [By the end of the project, at least 250 targeted engineering structures have been established to withstand the effects of GLOF events on livelihood assets].
2.4: 01 [By the end of the project, at least 01 community driven initiative in each of the 24 target valleys, is financed through CBDRM funds].</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.1: Scale 3 [The project strengthened provincial line departments through experts and technical assistance in GIS mapping, conducting HVRAs and ground-truthing. Climate change cells have been established at national and sub-national level. Initial assessments (Baseline assessment, KAP studies, HVRA etc.) have been carried out. The project helped strengthen the District Emergency Response Cells (DERC) through procurement and provision of emergency and response equipment valued at USD 700,000 to the Disaster Management Authorities in Khyber Pakhtunkhwa and Gilgit-Baltistan (GBDMA and KPDMA). Components through UNDP support and GOP are on track, however, some activities impacted by COVID]

1.2: 0 [Guidelines and SOPs have been drafted for establishing CBDRMCs Revolving Funds, that will ultimately feed into the developmental plans at provincial and national level to incorporate GLOF risks. CCA action plan for GB has been initiated and is in progress whereas the same process for KP will be initiated in first quarter of 2021]

2.1: 0 [No Early Warning system has been installed yet. Partial procurement of equipment initiated, the procurement and installation of the remaining EWS equipment will be carried out in 2021]

2.2: 0 [12 CBOs (5 in KP and 7 in GB) have been established. Under the project, a series of community meetings, awareness raising campaigns, coordination workshops were conducted to sensitize communities and key stakeholders about the impact of GLOFs. 7 CBOs have had preliminary trainings on the operation and maintenance of EWS. Formal training to CBOs on EWS O&amp;M will be provided once EWS is installed and functional] 

2.3: 0 [The groundwork (site selection, design and layout, BoQs) for physical assets has been completed. Construction of most of the infrastructural activities could not be initiated due to COVID-19 restrictions and later on extreme weather conditions, however contracts have been issued against 13 irrigation channels in KP] 

2.4: 0 [Guidelines were developed to finance the targeted communities in GB and KP, however, the project was later informed that the activity cannot be implemented as on-granting was not allowed under UNDPs accreditation status with the donor]</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.1: Scale 4 [By the end of 2022, 100% (MOCC and PMD) of the national and 100% of district authorities (P&amp;DD, Forest Department, Irrigation department, OFWM, EPA etc.) and communities in KP and GB will be able to prioritize and plan measures to minimize potential losses from GLOFs through capacity building for EWS operation and maintenance and for project interventions] 
1.2: 03 [By the end of 2022 at least 03 guidelines/strategy/policy (01 National Policy and 02 Provincial CCAAP) have been established/adopted by the Government to address or incorporate GLOF risk reduction]
2.1: 435,214 people [By the end of 2022, 15 GLOF valleys will have early warning systems installed in KP and GB and 62.5% of households in target communities are able to receive and respond to early warnings and take appropriate actions following the warning (217,607 men, 217,607 women] 
2.2: 24 [By the end of 2022, at least 24 CBOs would be trained in the operation and maintenance of the EWS and ensure its continued functionality]
2.3: 200/182/540ha [By the end of 2022, at least at least 200 targeted small-scale infrastructure, 540 Ha of slope stabilization and at least 182 irrigation schemes have been established/rehabilitated to withstand the effects of GLOF events on livelihood assets.
2.4: 0 [Formal approval is awaited from GCF on revoking of revolving fund activity from project scope]</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.1: Scale 3 [The strengthening of the institutions and departments both at Federal and Provincial level was done through capacity building trainings and stakeholder coordination workshops. In 2022, 58 trainings and workshops were completed with 2,340 participants (339 women and 2,001 men). These include - 08 community trainings on CC and GLOF Risk reduction with 338 participants (31 women, 307 men), 27 Mock drills with 1,385 participants (233 women and 810 men), 18 trainings on Climate Change Adaptation Plans (CCAP) with 748 participants (63 women and 685 men) and 05 stakeholder coordination workshops with 211 participants (12 women and 199 men).]
1.2: 02 [02 Climate Change Adaptation Action Plans (01 GB and 01 KP) were approved and adopted by the respective provincial Governments to address the GLOF risks, for integrated institutional and regulatory climate-responsive planning and development. In 2022, Project has also supported the development of 04 plans (02 monsoon and 02 winter contingency plans for GB and KP)].
2.1: 000 [EWS equipment could not be installed in 2022 due to late land acquisition for base platform construction by the provincial government. Installation will commence in 2023. Once EWS is installed in 2023, the communities will benefit by being able to receive and respond to early warnings.]
2.2: 0 [Trainings of 24 CBOs of the project valleys on operation and maintenance of EWS will be completed in 2023]. 
2.3: 67/55/400 [67 small scale infrastructure (against 200), 55 (against 182) irrigation channels and 400 Ha (against 540) slope stabilization activities have been completed and remaining are aligned for construction in 2023].
2.4: 0 [Decision granted by GCF in November 2022 for revoking of revolving fund activity from project scope. Activity Indicator has been revised in the project log frame approved by GCF in December 2022 and Project activities are aligned for 2023 for alternate livelihood support (kitchen gardening, development of manual for communities and financial institutions for alternate livelihood opportunities)].</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2021-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.1: Scale 4 [By the end of Year 3, 100% of the national and 90% of district and community authorities in the KP and GB regions are able to prioritize and plan measures to minimize potential losses from GLOFs]
1.2. 4 [By the end of the project, number of policies introduced to address GLOF risks or adjusted to incorporate GLOF risks]
2.1: 696,342 people [348,171 men, 348,171 women [Planned for end of project].
2.2: 24 [By the end of the project, at least 24 CBOs are trained in the operation and maintenance of the EWS and ensure its continued functionality].
2.3: 250 [By the end of the project, at least 250 targeted engineering structures have been established to withstand the effects of GLOF events on livelihood assets].
2.4: 01 [By the end of the project, at least 01 community driven initiative in each of the 24 target valleys, is financed through CBDRM funds].</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.1: Scale 3 [The strengthening of the institutions and departments both at Federal and Provincial level was done through trainings, workshops, capacity building for human resource, disaster risk reduction and disaster risk management. In 2021, 51 trainings were organized (community and stakeholders) in which 1609 participants attended (1080 men and 529 women), 71 community mobilization sessions with 2169 participants (1598 men and 571 women); 43 mock drills with 2138 participants (1395 men and 743 women) were conducted.]
1.2: 02 [This is Year 04 of the project with an extension until January 2024. The project has so far provided support and inputs for drafting of Climate Change Adaptation Action Plans for GB and KP. These will pave way for devising policy interventions for implementation of Climate Change Policy and Action Plans. The policies to be adopted to address the GLOF risks is linked to the institutional and regulatory systems for climate-responsive planning and development.].
2.1: 0 [The mapping of households and beneficiaries is available with project for target valleys, Once the EWS is installed, all will benefit from installed EWS.]
2.2: 0 [In all 24 valleys, Community Based Disaster Risk Management Committees (CBDRMCs) have been established. The mandate of these Committees is to implement and safeguard project interventions, specifically focusing on sustainability after project completion. The EWS will be installed in 2022 after which formal training for Operations and Maintenance of EWS to these CBDRMCs will be provided]. 
2.3: 28 [In 2021, the GIS mapping and HVRA studies for all 24 project valleys have been completed. 28 engineering structures (irrigation channels) have been completed in 2021 and remaining are aligned for construction in 2022.]
2.4: 0 [Revolving Fund activity is an on-granting activity and formal approval is awaited from GCF on revoking of this activity from project scope].</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
